- Method:
  - For pattern A (extension-class size): find sentences containing both a regularity class token (C2, C^2, H2_loc, C^{1,1}, C0) and a size/comparison token (larger, smaller, subset, innermost, outermost, strictly stronger/weaker, contains, contained). Classify direction: 
    - A-CORRECT if "C2" is described as smaller/subset/innermost/contained in a larger class, or "C0 larger/outermost"
    - A-INVERTED if "C2 ... larger"/"C0 ... smaller" as a *positive assertion*
    - A-NEGATED if the phrase is inside a negation/quote/error-report context (e.g., "[R2 major: ...]", "earlier revision listed", "contradiction") — must NOT be flagged as live error.
  - For pattern B (SET vs tail): find sentences mentioning "set-based" or "SET" variant and "strictly stronger/weaker" or "implies". Classify: B-INVERTED if "set-based ... strictly stronger" (positive assertion), B-CORRECT if "strictly weaker" or "single-q ... stronger".
  - Controls:
    - C1: a known-correct statement inside the same corpus must classify CORRECT (e.g., taxonomy:141 "meaning_C0 ... strictly stronger than the C2 conclusion" — correct direction).
    - C2: a synthetic inverted sentence must classify INVERTED; a synthetic corrected sentence must classify CORRECT; a negated meta-discussion must classify NEGATED (self-test harness).
    - C3: hash-pin the inputs and re-measure at the end (live drift detection).
  - Honest limitation: lexical classification of natural-language direction; report only explicit assertion sentences; every hit carries a ±160 char context quote so a reviewer can adjudicate.

